
 Boot and reset messages
The main boot line is:

BOOT V3.6 reset=<reason>(<number>) prevLoopStage=<n>

For example, reset=SW(3) prevLoopStage=7 means the ESP32 reports a software reset; 3 is the numeric reset-reason enum. prevLoopStage=7 is a separate value: the loop stage recorded before setup began. It is not part of the reset reason.

The reset reason names handled by the code are:

POWERON — power-on reset
EXT — external reset
SW — software reset
PANIC — software panic
INT_WDT — interrupt watchdog
TASK_WDT — task watchdog
WDT — other watchdog reset
DEEPSLEEP — wake from deep sleep
BROWNOUT — supply voltage dropped too low
OTHER — any reason not listed
After boot, the sketch may also log:

Watchdog Reset s<n> — specifically identifies a task-watchdog reset and includes the saved loop stage.
Software Reset — generic restart event for a software reset.
System Restart — generic event for other restart reasons.
A normal power-on does not add one of those restart events.
Loop-stage reference (the value is set just before each stage runs):

Stage	Code being run
1	Environment history rollover
2	Inside sensor update
3	Outside sensor update
4	Rotary/manual switch handling
5	Event/environment page input
6	Door movement and limit-switch update
7	Door obstruction/safety cycle
8	Motor safety check
9	Buzzer pattern
10	Status LEDs
11	Night LEDs
12	Post-close light hold
13	Wi-Fi connection handling
14	Display refresh
15	Manual-light timeout, OLED sleep and once-a-minute work
16	Dashboard web server
17	OTA handler
A saved stage is a clue, not proof of the cause: it says where the loop last recorded progress, not necessarily what caused the reset. Stage 15 covers several different tasks. On a power-on, loopStage may not contain a meaningful previous value because it is marked RTC_NOINIT_ATTR.

Startup, sensors and storage
SHT31 initialised at address 0x45 — inside sensor setup.
SHT30 OUT initialised at address 0x44 — outside sensor setup, if enabled.
Loaded history_in / Loaded outside history — saved history was found and loaded.
No inside history found / No outside history found — no correctly sized saved history was found.
RTC OK — RTC was found and its stored date looked valid.
RTC NOT FOUND — RTC could not be detected.
RTC INVALID — RTC date was outside the accepted range.
RTC LOST POWER and the event RTC Battery Low — RTC reports that it lost power.
RTC synced from NTP — RTC was updated from network time.
NTP Timeout — the sketch did not get network time within the startup wait.
Time synced — valid network time was obtained.
Environment date initialised — first-time saved date tracking was set up.
Environment date: clock moved backwards — current date is earlier than the saved date, so rollover was withheld.
Environment log date invalid — saved date failed calendar validation.
Environment rollover: NVS open failed — non-volatile storage could not be opened for rollover.
Environment log rolled — daily climate history was rolled over.
Daily history backup saved (in + out) — daily history backup was written to storage.
Sensor-log caveat: the two sensor failure messages are incomplete in the supplied code: SHT3x Read Invalid (attempts= and SHT30 OUT Read Invalid temp= are printed without the promised values or a newline. They may run into the next log message instead of appearing as complete lines.

Wi-Fi, dashboard and OTA
WiFi Connected / WiFi Lost — connection-state events.
An IP address by itself — printed when Wi-Fi connects.
WiFi Disabled — retry limit was reached; Wi-Fi retries are paused until the retry logic runs again.
Dashboard: http://<IP> — dashboard address on the local network.
OTA ready: <hostname> at <IP> — OTA listener started.
OTA: update starting — OTA upload began.
OTA: <percent>% — approximate upload progress.
OTA: complete, rebooting — update completed; the device is rebooting.
OTA: error - <reason> — upload failed. Reasons include wrong password, begin failure, connection failure, interrupted transfer, or rejected image.
Door, light and safety events
These are event messages written to Serial and the Coop Log:

Door UNKNOWN, Door OPEN, Homing CLOSE, Home OK — startup door-position recovery.
Door Open <seconds>s / Door Close <seconds>s — door reached its limit; includes travel time.
Auto Open / Auto Close — automatic door command was issued.
Manual OPEN / Manual CLOSE — manual door command.
Close Obstruction — door failed to close within its normal motor timeout; recovery was started.
Auto_Safety — the automation state machine entered safety recovery.
Safety timeout - obstruction unresolved — safety recovery exceeded its timeout.
Door blocked — obstruction retries were exhausted.
OPEN timeout / CLOSE timeout — door did not reach the corresponding limit before its motor timeout.
HOME FAIL — startup homing did not reach the close limit in time.
LIMIT CONFLICT — both limit switches appeared active.
MOTOR CONFLICT — both motor direction outputs appeared active.
OPEN LIMIT FAIL / CLOSE LIMIT FAIL — repeated limit-related faults triggered a lockout.
FAULT, followed by the specific fault reason — fault recorded.
SYSTEM LOCKOUT, followed by its reason — system lockout triggered.
Fault Cleared — fault state was cleared.
Auto_Idle, Auto_Allowed, Auto_Locked — automation state changed.
Cold Delay — scheduled morning opening was postponed due to cold.
Light ON / Light OFF — automatic lighting events.
Manual Light ON / Manual Light OFF — manual light control.
Light Timeout — manual light-on timer expired.
RESET OK — fault/reset action completed.
Time failed — the once-a-minute check found no valid time.
No time since <HH:MM> / No valid time — prolonged loss of valid time triggered the alarm.


